## KEYWORDS
metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), cadmium sulfide, core–shell, photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation, formic acid, selectivity
## ABSTRACT
Encapsulating well-defined nanoparticles (NPs) into metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) to form core–shell structures not only combines their properties, but may also provide synergistic functionality. Much research attention has been focused on applying core–shell NP@MOF structures to gas storage and sensing, luminescence, lithium ion batteries, selective catalysis, and cascade reactions; however, their application as photocatalysts has been reported much more rarely. Herein, we report the design of a new core–shell structure composed of semiconductor CdS NPs as the core and ZIF-8 as the shell. Both single-core–shell and multiple-core–shell CdS@ZIF-8 were synthesized by varying the concentrations of the ZIF-8 precursor. Photocatalytic hydrogen generation from formic acid over CdS@ZIF-8 demonstrated that core–shell CdS@ZIF-8 structures exhibit increased photocatalytic selectivity for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation from formic acid compared with pure CdS NPs. Furthermore, the concentration of CO in the products was significantly decreased, which benefits the application of CdS@ZIF-8 to proton-exchange membrane fuel cells. Our results provide guidance for the development of new photocatalysts based on NP@MOF core–shell materials.
## 1 Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which consist of metal ions (or clusters) and organic bridging ligands, have attracted considerable research attention owing to their tunable pore size, high pore volume, high specific surface area, and highly ordered crystalline
porous networks [1, 2]. In recent years, the encapsulation of well-defined nanoparticles (NPs) into MOFs to form core–shell structures has been extensively investigated [3, 4], and MOFs have been successfully combined with a series of active species, including metal NPs [5–8], metal oxides [9–11], organic polymers [12, 13], polyoxometalates (POMs) [14, 15], and MOFs
[16, 17]. These hybrid materials exhibit not only the intrinsic properties of the MOFs and NPs from which they are constructed, but also synergistic properties that have allowed their application in new fields, such as gas storage and sensing [18, 19], therapy [20, 21], lithium ion batteries [22], selective catalysis [23, 24], and cascade reactions [25]. Furthermore, the use of semiconductors as photocatalysts is considered to be a promising approach to addressing environmental issues and the energy crisis [26–28]. However, employing photocatalysts as cores in core–shell NP@MOF nanostructures has been reported only rarely.
Formic acid is regarded as a potential carrier for hydrogen production owing to its high gravimetric capacity \((4.4\%)\) and abundance in biomass [29]. The decomposition of formic acid may proceed via two pathways [30]: dehydrogenation, which generates \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(C O_{2},\) and dehydration, which generates \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and CO. However, CO poisons proton-exchange membrane fuel cells [31]. Therefore, reducing the generation of CO from formic acid decomposition, i.e., increasing the selectivity of formic acid decomposition towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation, is of great importance.
In this study, we have synthesized well-defined \(\mathrm{CdS}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) core–shell structures by a two-step method, in which polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP)-stabilized CdS NPs were synthesized and ZIF-8 shells were grown on the surfaces of the CdS cores. The thickness of the MOF shell can be tailored from \(102\pm5.4\ \mathrm{nm}\) to \(13.6\,\pm\,2.5\,\mathrm{nm}\) by varying the concentration of the 2-methylimidazole (HMeIM) precursor. The core–shell \(\mathrm{CdS}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) particles produced were applied to photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation from formic acid, and exhibited improved selectivity for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation compared with pure CdS nanoparticles. Correspondingly, the molar percentage of CO generated was decreased significantly from \(12.5\%\) to \(3.2\%\) .
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of PVP-stabilized CdS
PVP-stabilized CdS nanoparticles were synthesized according to a previously report [32]. Typically, \(\mathrm{Cd(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O}\) \((2.16\;\mathrm{g},\;7.0\;\mathrm{mmol})\) , thiourea \((0.53\;\mathrm{g},\) \(7.0\;\mathrm{mmol})\) , PVP \((0.78\;\mathrm{g},\,7.0\;\mathrm{mmol},\) , calculated in terms of the repeating unit \(\mathrm{C_{6}H_{9}N O\ F_{W}}=111\) ), and ethylene
glycol \((70\,\mathrm{mL})\) were mixed and ultrasonicated until homogeneous. Then, the mixture was transferred to a \(100\,\mathrm{mL}\) PTFE-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(4\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . CdS NPs with a size of \(70\,\mathrm{nm}\) were precipitated with acetone, collected by centrifugation at \(10,000\;\mathrm{rpm},\) and washed with deionized water and methanol. Finally, the obtained CdS NPs were dispersed in \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) methanol \((0.25\;\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) . CdS NPs with a diameter of \(210\;\mathrm{nm}\) were prepared using the same procedure, except that the reaction time was increased to \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) .
## 2.2 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{CdS}@Z\mathbf{IF}{-}8\) core–shell nanostructures
The CdS NPs solution \((2\,\mathrm{mL},0.25\,\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) was added to a solution of HMeIM in methanol \((10\,\mathrm{mL},\,10\,\mathrm{mM})\) . The solution was ultrasonicated for \(15\,\mathrm{{min},}\) , and \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) of \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) in methanol \((10\,\mathrm{mM})\) was added and allowed to react at room temperature for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) without stirring. The product was separated by centrifugation \((10,000\;\mathrm{rpm},\,10\,\mathrm{min})\) , washed two times with methanol, and vacuum-dried overnight at \(100\,^{\circ}\!C\) .
## 2.3 Characterization of \(\mathbf{CdS}@Z\mathbf{IF}{-}8\) core–shell structures
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained on a Hitachi S-4800 at an operating voltage of \(10\,\mathrm{kV}.\) Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were taken on an FEI Tecnai T20 at an operating voltage of \(200\;\mathrm{kV}.\) High-angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADFSTEM) imaging and energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) elemental mapping were performed on an FEI Tecnai F30. X-ray diffraction (XRD) characterization was performed on a Rigaku D/max-2800 diffractometer with \(\mathrm{Cu}\;\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda=0.1542\;\mathrm{nm})\) using a current of \(100\,\mathrm{mA}\) and a voltage of \(40\,\mathrm{kV}.\)
## 2.4 Photocatalytic tests
Photocatalytic experiments were performed in a \(38\,\mathrm{mL}\) Pyrex tube at \(293\;\mathrm{K}\) under Ar atmosphere. A mixture of HCOOH \((3.0\;\mathrm{mmol})\) , HCOONa \((1.0\;\mathrm{mmol})\) , photocatalyst \((5\mathrm{mg})\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((22.5\;\upmu\mathrm{L},\,2.0\;\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) and deionized water \((5.0\;\mathrm{mL})\) were placed into the tube, which was degassed by freeze-pump-thaw cycling. The mixture was then irradiated using a Xe lamp fitted with a \(420\;\mathrm{nm}\) cutoff filter under magnetic stirring.
After the reaction, the generated gases were analyzed by gas chromatography (Techcomp 7900). It should be noted that \(20\,\mathrm{mL}\) of the CdS NP solution \((0.25\,\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) was employed to synthesize CdS@ZIF-8; thus, the amount of CdS in all the photocatalysts was the same \((5.0\;\mathrm{mg})\).
## 3 Results and discussion
The synthesized single-core–shell structures designated by \(\mathsf{S C S}_{-x}\), where \(x\) refers to the concentration of HMeIM. For example, SCS-10 indicates that the concentration of HMeIM employed was \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\). The structure and morphology of SCS-10 were characterized by SEM, TEM, XRD, and EDX elemental mapping (Fig. 1). The SEM and TEM images show that the obtained material has a single-core–shell structure and its shell thickness is \(102\pm5.4\,\mathrm{nm}\). EDX elemental mapping was performed to study the spatial distribution of Cd, S, and Zn. These images indicate that the Cd and the majority of the S are distributed in the core of the NP, and the Zn is homogenously distributed throughout the whole NP, suggesting that the CdS core is well encapsulated by the ZIF-8 shell. All the peaks in the XRD pattern of CdS@ZIF-8 can be attributed to ZIF-8 and hexagonal phase CdS. The diffraction peaks at \(2\theta=24.9^{\circ}\), \(26.5^{\circ}\), \(28.2^{\circ}\), \(43.8^{\circ}\), \(47.8^{\circ}\), and \(51.9^{\circ}\) correspond to the (100), (002), (101), (110), (103), and (112) crystal planes of CdS (PDF#65–3414), respectively. The diffraction peaks at \(2\theta=7.4^{\circ}\), \(10.4^{\circ}\), \(12.8^{\circ}\), \(14.7^{\circ}\), \(16.5^{\circ}\), and \(18.1^{\circ}\) correspond to the (011), (002), (112), (022), (013), and (222) crystal planes of ZIF-8, respectively. The encapsulation process was further investigated by changing the concentration of the ZIF-8 precursors (Fig. 2). When the concentration of HMeIM is increased from 10 to \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\), the shell thickness decreases from \(102\pm5.4\,\mathrm{nm}\) to \(35\pm3.1\,\mathrm{nm}\) (SCS-20), while the morphologies remain unchanged. Here, the excess HMeIM may act not only as a base for the deprotonation of the bridging ligands, but also as a capping agent capable of inhibiting ZIF-8 crystalline growth [33, 34]. Hence, the nucleation rate is accelerated and nuclei growth is restricted, resulting in decreased shell thickness. However, multiple-core–shell structures (MCSs) with uniform size and shape are obtained when the concentration of \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) is increased from 10 to \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\). The increase in the \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\)
concentration may accelerate the growth of ZIF-8. Thus, the CdS NPs are encapsulated in the ZIF-8 crystals to produce the multiple-core–shell structures. The encapsulation process was further investigated by employing two different sized CdS cores (Fig. S1
On the basis of our study, two encapsulation processes for CdS NPs into ZIF-8 are proposed, as shown in Fig. 4. For the encapsulation of MCSs (entry 1), a large number of ZIF-8 crystal nuclei are first adsorbed on the surface of PVP-stabilized CdS. Then, the coalescence of the nuclei occurs, and the ZIF-8 grows homogeneously. The PVP-stabilized CdS NPs are successively adsorbed on the continuously forming surfaces of the homogeneously growing ZIF-8 crystal. Finally, the multiple CdS NPs are well encapsulated by a single ZIF-8 crystal. For the encapsulation of the SCSs (entry 2), the PVP-stabilized CdS acts as a seed for the heterogeneous nucleation of the ZIF-8 shell on its surface, forming a continuous coverage of polycrystalline ZIF-8. When the amount of CdS NPs is insufficient, ZIF-8 precursors grow homogeneously into single ZIF-8 crystals.
The photocatalytic decomposition of formic acid was conducted to investigate the synergistic effect of the CdS and ZIF-8 in the core–shell nanostructures. Pure CdS NPs, MCS, SCS-20, and SCS-40 were used as the photocatalysts in the formic acid decomposition, and the resulting photocatalytic kinetic profiles are shown in Fig. 5. Compared with pure CdS NPs, the MCSs exhibit higher photocatalytic activity for \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) generation over the whole photocatalytic process, but SCS-20 shows higher photocatalytic activity for \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) generation only in the first \( 3.5\,\mathrm{h} \), and SCS-40 shows higher photocatalytic activity for \( \mathrm{H}_{2} \) generation only in the first \( 2.5\,\mathrm{h} \). Furthermore, the MCSs, SCS-20, and SCS-40 show lower photocatalytic activities for CO
generation compared with pure CdS NPs. As shown in Fig. 6, the selectivity for CO when employing the MCSs, SCS–20, and SCS-40 decreases to \(5.7\%\) , \(5.8\%\) , and \(3.2\%\) , respectively. Therefore, encapsulating CdS NPs in a ZIF-8 shell improves the photocatalytic selectivity for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation from formic acid. Because the kinetic diameter of CO \((3.8\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) is larger than the size of the narrow windows \((3.4\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) , CO cannot diffuse into the pores of ZIF-8. As a result, CO generation is hindered, and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation is promoted. In addition, the decrease in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation with SCS-20 and SCS-40 is possibly due to decomposition of the ZIF-8.
## 4 Conclusions
We have reported for the first time in the literature the synthesis of well-designed core–shell semiconductor@MOF materials with different morphologies and shell thickness. Core–shell structures with two distinct morphologies were prepared by varying the concentration of the ZIF-8 precursors. We have proposed a growth mechanism for each morphology, i.e., one involving the successive adsorption of PVP-stabilized CdS on the surfaces of the growing ZIF-8; and one in which CdS acts as a seed and allows the heterogeneous nucleation of ZIF-8 shells on its surface. Furthermore, photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation from formic acid using the core–shell materials as photocatalysts exhibited higher selectivity for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) than the pure CdS NPs. This study provides some guidance for the development of new photocatalysts based on NP@MOF core–shell materials.
